The figure illustrates that Nano-11 nanoparticles are actively transported to the draining lymph nodes by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) after injection, with the percentage of Nano-11-containing myeloid cells peaking at Day 2. Notably, migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) are the predominant APC subtype responsible for this uptake, internalizing a higher proportion of Nano-11-positive cells and exhibiting a significantly greater mean fluorescence intensity compared to lymphoid tissue resident DCs and monocytes/macrophages. This indicates MigDCs are key in trafficking Nano-11 to the lymph nodes.